    "bodyHtml": "<p>ITZY's Chaeryeong is finally center stage on her own: her <strong>first mini album \"Lovesick\" dropped November 9, 2026</strong>, with the bubbly title-track MV arriving the same day — making her the <strong>third ITZY member to go solo</strong>, roughly seven years and nine months after the group's debut. And she didn't take the safe route: this is an <strong>easy-listening</strong> debut from a member of the industry's most high-energy girl group. Here's the full breakdown — as of November 10, 2026.</p>\n\n<h2>Key details</h2>\n\n<table>\n  <tr><th><strong>Detail</strong></th><th><strong>Info</strong></th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Album</td><td>Lovesick (1st mini album)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Release date</td><td>November 9, 2026</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Tracks</td><td>4 — \"four sides of falling in love\"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Title track</td><td>\"Lovesick\"</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Genre direction</td><td>Easy-listening — a deliberate left turn</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Milestone</td><td>Third ITZY solo (after Yeji and Yuna), ~7 yrs 9 mo after debut</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Solo confirmed</td><td>August 27, 2026 (Soompi)</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<h2>The ITZY solo trilogy — where Chaeryeong fits</h2>\n\n<p>JYP has rolled out ITZY's solo chapter one member at a time, and each pick made a statement:</p>\n\n<table>\n  <tr><th><strong>Order</strong></th><th><strong>Member</strong></th><th><strong>The statement</strong></th></tr>\n  <tr><td>First</td><td>Yeji</td><td>The leader steps out first — the obvious-power move</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Second</td><td>Yuna</td><td>The youngest claims her own lane</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Third</td><td><strong>Chaeryeong</strong></td><td>The performance main takes the boldest musical swing</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>Chaeryeong herself described the position with baseball honesty at her press conference: <strong>\"I'm ITZY's No. 3 hitter\"</strong> — swinging third in the solo order, with the most context behind her and the biggest expectations on her at-bat.</p>\n\n<h2>The rollout — from August confirmation to release</h2>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>August 27:</strong> solo debut confirmed by JYP (Soompi)</li>\n  <li><strong>Mid-October:</strong> promo schedule and tracklist — four tracks, \"four sides of falling in love\"</li>\n  <li><strong>November 4:</strong> second MV teaser</li>\n  <li><strong>November 6:</strong> highlight medley previewing all four tracks</li>\n  <li><strong>November 9:</strong> <strong>Lovesick releases</strong> + title-track MV + press conference the same day</li>\n  <li><strong>November 10:</strong> FLO \"Cheer\" fan-support campaign lights up around the release</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>The songs — four sides of falling in love</h2>\n\n<p>The four tracks trace falling in love across its moods, with the title track's MV going warm and bubbly rather than dramatic — a fitting visual for an album built on gentle hooks rather than drops. The genre gamble is the story: <strong>easy-listening</strong>, from a member of a group whose catalog is built on maximalist, high-impact performance tracks.</p>\n\n<p>At the November 9 press conference, Chaeryeong was disarmingly honest about the choice — she picked easy-listening precisely because <em>\"you never know where a challenge will take off.\"</em> Translation: the safe solo debut was another dance-pop single; the interesting one was showing a different voice entirely.</p>\n\n<h2>The quotes that made the press conference</h2>\n\n<p>Korean media ran with several lines from Chaeryeong's November 9 session:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>On her held-back dance lines: she admitted she'd <strong>\"practiced until I got a crick in my neck\"</strong> — years of restraining the dance volume that ITZY's group choreography didn't need, finally unleashed on her own stages</li>\n  <li>On her concept ambition: she wants to <strong>\"bewitch like a Gumiho\"</strong> — the Korean fox-spirit charm offensive</li>\n  <li>On the batting order: <strong>\"I'm ITZY's No. 3 hitter\"</strong> — the veteran slot in the solo trilogy</li>\n  <li>On craft: her respect for <strong>JYP founder Park Jin-young's</strong> musical instincts shaped how she approached the album</li>\n  <li>And the family thread: her sister <strong>Lee Chae-yeon</strong> — herself a former idol — offered advice through the process, the kind of counsel only another idol sibling can give</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Why an easy-listening debut is a genuine gamble</h2>\n\n<p>Solo debuts from performance-group members usually play it safe: a dance single, a strong beat, a version of the group sound with one face. Chaeryeong went the opposite way — <strong>no noise, nowhere to hide, everything riding on tone and feel.</strong> Easy-listening lives or dies on vocal texture and emotional restraint, and it asks the audience to lean in rather than get hit. Early coverage framed it as the performance queen returning on her own terms — and as the boldest sonic left-turn of ITZY's three solos.</p>\n\n<p>Her ask to fans was classic idol charm with a Chaeryeong twist: watch the MV <strong>once a day for two weeks</strong> — and MIDZY delivered, with the FLO \"Cheer\" fan-support campaign lighting up around release day.</p>\n\n<h2>What to watch next</h2>\n\n<p>First-week chart numbers, the music-show stages (where those held-back dance lines finally get full volume), and whether \"Lovesick\" carves the easy-listening lane wider for idol solos generally. If it works, expect the \"soft debut\" playbook to get copied across the industry — and Chaeryeong gets credited as the one who tried it first.</p>\n\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n\n<h3>When did Chaeryeong debut solo?</h3>\n<p>November 9, 2026, with the mini album \"Lovesick\" and the title-track MV.</p>\n\n<h3>How many songs are on Lovesick?</h3>\n<p>Four tracks — framed as \"four sides of falling in love.\"</p>\n\n<h3>What genre is the album?</h3>\n<p>Easy-listening — a deliberate departure from ITZY's high-energy signature, chosen by Chaeryeong as a personal challenge.</p>\n\n<h3>Which ITZY members released solo albums before her?</h3>\n<p>Yeji and Yuna — making Chaeryeong the third member to go solo, the \"No. 3 hitter\" in her own words.</p>\n\n<h3>Was there a press conference?</h3>\n<p>Yes, on release day (November 9) — where she discussed the easy-listening direction, the \"Gumiho\" concept ambition, her neck-crick practice honesty, and advice from sister Lee Chae-yeon.</p>\n\n<h3>How long after ITZY's debut is this?</h3>\n<p>Roughly seven years and nine months — a long-waited solo that fans had requested for years.</p>\n\n<p><em>Sourced from JYP Entertainment, 매일경제, Chosunbiz, allkpop, Soompi, Star News and FLO campaign coverage. Accurate as of November 10, 2026.</em></p>",
