    "bodyHtml": "<p>While London got July, Taiwan gets <strong>November</strong>: BTS's WORLD TOUR 'ARIRANG' stops in <strong>Kaohsiung this month for three concerts</strong> — the tour's Taiwan debut era and one of the biggest live-music events the city has ever hosted. The city knows it, the fans know it, and even the temples know it (more on that shortly). Here's what's confirmed, what the ticket situation really looks like, and how to plan a trip around it — as of November 10, 2026.</p>\n\n<h2>The basics</h2>\n\n<table>\n  <tr><th><strong>Detail</strong></th><th><strong>Status</strong></th></tr>\n  <tr><td>City</td><td>Kaohsiung, Taiwan</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Shows</td><td>Three concerts</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Month</td><td>November 2026 (this month)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Announced</td><td>January 14, 2026 (Taiwan News)</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Exact dates &amp; venue door times</td><td>Confirm via official promoter channels</td></tr>\n</table>\n\n<p>The three-show run was announced on January 14, 2026 — one day after the world tour itself was revealed — as part of the Asia leg whose venues and on-sale details were confirmed on May 22. For Taiwanese ARMY, it's the group's first full-group Taiwan stop of the post-service era, and demand has matched the moment.</p>\n\n<h2>The ticket story: queues, prayers and national news</h2>\n\n<p>Kaohsiung's June on-sale became a story in itself. With demand far outstripping supply, Taiwanese ARMY went viral for visiting temples to <strong>pray to the God of Love for ticketing luck</strong> — WION, Taiwan News and Interaksyon all covered the queues, turning the on-sale into national news. That's the honest baseline: <strong>assume face-value tickets are gone.</strong></p>\n\n<p>What's actually left, in order of realism:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Official production releases</strong> — final seat releases sometimes appear close to show dates through the official ticketing partner. Watch the promoter's official channels only.</li>\n  <li><strong>Authorised resale</strong> — if the Taiwanese promoter designated an official resale platform, that's the only resale worth touching.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cinema live-viewing</strong> — the ARIRANG tour broadcasts to roughly 6,100 cinemas worldwide, and Taiwan's cinema chains have participated in the tour's live-viewing dates through 2026. Big screen, full ARMY crowd, a fraction of the stress.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>Kaohsiung is rolling out the red carpet</h2>\n\n<p>The city government is actively courting concertgoers — which makes even a ticketless trip worthwhile:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Special offers for BTS concertgoers</strong> announced by Kaohsiung (Taipei Times, November 9) — discounts and perks tied to the concert dates</li>\n  <li><strong>City-wide food promotions</strong> timed to the shows (Taiwan News, November 10) — restaurants across the city joining in</li>\n  <li><strong>A measurable travel surge</strong> — Agoda reported a spike in international travel interest to both Kaohsiung and Taipei around the BTS and BIGBANG concert windows</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Getting there and around — practical specifics</h2>\n\n<p>Kaohsiung is one of the easiest stadium-cities in Asia to navigate:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Flying in:</strong> Kaohsiung International Airport (KHH) serves regional routes directly — often cheaper and faster than flying into Taipei for a Kaohsiung event.</li>\n  <li><strong>From Taipei:</strong> the THSR high-speed rail runs Taipei → Zuoying in roughly 90–120 minutes; Zuoying station connects onward to the city's metro (KRTC) and the stadium district. This is the standard fan route.</li>\n  <li><strong>Show nights:</strong> expect packed KRTC trains and gridlocked roads near the venue for hours. Travel early, eat early, and buy a metro card on day one — it works on buses too.</li>\n  <li><strong>Three-show warning:</strong> three consecutive stadium-scale crowds mean queues everywhere — build buffer time into everything from hotel check-in to dinner.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Hotels: the January cautionary tale</h2>\n\n<p>One thing to know before booking: Kaohsiung hotels were <strong>accused of overcharging</strong> around the concert dates back in January (Taiwan News), and the city <strong>investigated price gouging</strong> (Taipei Times, January 18). Local authorities took the complaints seriously — but the smart-play rules still apply:</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li>Compare rates across platforms before booking — screenshot the differences</li>\n  <li>Book refundable where possible until the official dates/doors are confirmed from the promoter</li>\n  <li>Ignore unofficial \"ARIRANG packages\" — the only packages worth buying come from the city's own announced offers</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>If you're going ticketless: the Kaohsiung game plan</h2>\n\n<p>Honestly? Kaohsiung might be the best city on the tour to be ticketless:</p>\n\n<ol>\n  <li>Book the trip — flights into KHH and a central hotel near the metro</li>\n  <li>Hit the city's announced food promotions and concertgoer offers</li>\n  <li>Soak up show-day atmosphere around the venue district (fans, photo ops, merch pop-ups if present)</li>\n  <li>Book a cinema live-viewing seat at a local chain for the full-production experience</li>\n  <li>Stay for the city itself — Kaohsiung's harbor, night markets and riverside make it a proper trip, not just a concert run</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>FAQ</h2>\n\n<h3>How many BTS concerts are in Kaohsiung?</h3>\n<p>Three, in November 2026 — announced January 14, 2026, one day after the world tour reveal.</p>\n\n<h3>Where exactly are the shows held?</h3>\n<p>Kaohsiung is the confirmed city; venue specifics and door times are published by the official promoter. Verify there before booking anything non-refundable.</p>\n\n<h3>Did tickets sell out?</h3>\n<p>The June on-sale saw extreme demand — the temple-praying queues became national news. Assume face-value tickets are gone and stick to authorised channels.</p>\n\n<h3>Is Kaohsiung doing anything special for fans?</h3>\n<p>Yes — the city announced special offers and food promotions for BTS concertgoers in November, and hotels and attractions across the city are leaning into the concert week.</p>\n\n<h3>How do I get to Kaohsiung from Taipei?</h3>\n<p>THSR high-speed rail from Taipei to Zuoying — roughly 90–120 minutes — then the KRTC metro onward. Book train seats early for show days.</p>\n\n<h3>What should I avoid when booking?</h3>\n<p>Unofficial \"ticket + hotel\" bundles, DM sellers, and resale outside authorised platforms. Kaohsiung authorities were already investigating hotel overcharging around the dates in January.</p>\n\n<h3>Is a ticketless trip actually worth it?</h3>\n<p>Genuinely yes — the city-wide offers, food promotions, show-day atmosphere and the cinema live-viewing make it a full fan experience, not a consolation trip.</p>\n\n<p><em>Sourced from Taiwan News, Taipei Times, WION, Interaksyon, Agoda reporting and official tour announcements. Accurate as of November 10, 2026.</em></p>",
